{"id": "2357c20d-6eeb-4303-8a57-03747cd1edd8", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"id": "tooluse_oWVTCPGJK572PaYyJT59oa", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Read-only resilience/DR inventory of a specific workload's three named resources in eu-central-1, to feed a disaster-recovery strategy review.",
  "context": "User named three resources believed to form an application backend: dr-demo-mysql (likely Amazon RDS for MySQL), dr-demo-valkey (likely Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey), dr-demo-players (likely a compute service - could be ECS, EC2/Auto Scaling, or Lambda - serving player/customer/purchase data). Region is eu-central-1, AWS account 012345678901_1. The business target is RPO 15 minutes / RTO 1 hour for customer and purchase data (the database is the source of truth). The cache (dr-demo-valkey) has been confirmed by the user as rebuildable from the database, so it does not need cross-region data protection itself - we just need to know if it can be recreated in a recovery region and whether the app tolerates a cold cache. Do NOT invent or assume facts; report only what read-only AWS calls return, and mark anything denied or empty as 'not checked'.",
  "resources": ["dr-demo-mysql", "dr-demo-valkey", "dr-demo-players", "eu-central-1"],
  "timeframe": "point-in-time inventory, no time window needed",
  "criteria": "Return ONE ROW PER RESOURCE in this exact column order: layer | resource | type | Region | in-Region redundancy (AZs, nodes, tasks) | backups (kind, frequency, retention) | copies/images/IaC outside Region | point-in-time recovery | replication | what could not be read. Specifically gather: (1) RDS: rds:DescribeDBInstances for dr-demo-mysql (Engine, MultiAZ, BackupRetentionPeriod, ReadReplicaDBInstanceIdentifiers, DeletionProtection, StorageEncrypted, KmsKeyId), rds:DescribeDBInstanceAutomatedBackups (any replicated automated backups in another region), rds:DescribeDBSnapshots (manual/automated, locations), rds:DescribeSourceRegions (is eu-central-1 able to receive/send cross-region automated backups, and to/from which regions - this tells us viable recovery regions). (2) ElastiCache: elasticache:DescribeReplicationGroups for dr-demo-valkey (member/node count, AutomaticFailover, MultiAZ, SnapshotRetentionLimit, GlobalReplicationGroupInfo, node type/family), elasticache:DescribeSnapshots. (3) Compute for dr-demo-players: identify what AWS resource type it actually is (check ecs:DescribeServices/DescribeTaskDefinition, ec2:DescribeInstances+DescribeAutoScalingGroups, or lambda:GetFunction as applicable), and report desired/running count, which AZs/subnets it spans, where its deployable artifact (container image or AMI) lives and whether that artifact exists outside eu-central-1. (4) Entry/routing: any elbv2 load balancer, apigateway API, or route53 hosted zone/health check in front of this app - report its AZs and whether any failover routing exists. (5) Shared dependencies: secretsmanager:DescribeSecret for any secrets these resources use (replica regions?), KMS key regions for encrypted RDS/ElastiCache, the VPC/subnets/NAT gateway setup (is there a zonal single point of failure?), and if compute uses ECR, ecr:DescribeRegistry replication config. (6) AWS Backup: backup:ListBackupPlans, backup:ListProtectedResources, backup:ListRecoveryPointsByResource for dr-demo-mysql and dr-demo-players (note AWS Backup does NOT support ElastiCache), backup:DescribeRegionSettings. Also note: do not pick a DR recovery region yourself - just report what data points toward one (e.g. which regions rds:DescribeSourceRegions allows, any existing cross-region copies)."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Inventory dr-demo-mysql, dr-demo-valkey, dr-demo-players resilience config in eu-central-1"}], "created_at": null}

